WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:09.013
This is Chapter 3: joiner workflows as
code, and the Temporary Access Pass bridge.

3
00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:12.687
A workflow edited in the portal
is a change nobody reviewed.

4
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:16.148
A workflow kept as a file
is one a reviewer can read,

5
00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:20.451
a test can lint, and a plan can
turn into exactly one new version.

6
00:00:20.451 --> 00:00:25.092
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's
procedure, then see what the kit adds:

7
00:00:25.092 --> 00:00:29.413
the workflow files, a lint, a plan,
and a measure of day-one readiness.

8
00:00:29.413 --> 00:00:32.120
Then two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.457
Why does this gate need code?

10
00:00:37.457 --> 00:00:41.104
The guide's Step 3 asks for
few workflows scoped by code,

11
00:00:41.104 --> 00:00:44.995
a pass that bridges to the card,
baseline access by package,

12
00:00:44.995 --> 00:00:50.182
execution limits on every scheduled
workflow, and a measured readiness rate.

13
00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:53.596
Each of those is a property
of a workflow definition.

14
00:00:53.596 --> 00:00:58.245
And Learn makes a definition change a new
version rather than an in-place update.

15
00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:00.418
That's what makes code the right home:

16
00:01:00.418 --> 00:01:03.530
the file is the version,
and the plan is the diff.

17
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.693
Here's what Learn gives you.

18
00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:12.156
The Create workflow request: a category,

19
00:01:12.156 --> 00:01:15.830
a name, flags, a scope,
a trigger and the tasks.

20
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:19.380
A changed definition goes to
the create new version action.

21
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.370
Triggers run on the hire date, the
leave date or the creation date,

22
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.080
within a hundred and
eighty days either way.

23
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.345
The pass task takes a lifetime
and a one-time-use flag,

24
00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:33.142
and Learn recommends at most
twenty-four hours for a new hire.

25
00:01:33.142 --> 00:01:36.675
And execution limits
quarantine a scheduled run,

26
00:01:36.675 --> 00:01:39.880
though a workflow's
limit isn't in the API.

27
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.993
What does Learn leave to you?

28
00:01:44.993 --> 00:01:48.230
It gives one request at
a time, written by hand.

29
00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:51.689
It doesn't keep the definitions
where a reviewer approves them,

30
00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:55.081
check a definition against its
own limits before the request,

31
00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:59.405
decide between create and new version,
or measure what the workflows achieved.

32
00:01:59.405 --> 00:02:02.770
So the kit keeps one file
per workflow, lints it,

33
00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.905
plans create or new version, and counts
readiness, each pinned by a test.

34
00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:12.041
The verdict: keep Learn's bodies
exactly as Learn shapes them,

35
00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:14.960
and add the file, lint and plan in front.

36
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.860
The kit holds six workflow files.

37
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.669
Two joiners: a pre-hire workflow that
sends the pass seven days before day one,

38
00:02:25.669 --> 00:02:29.485
and a day-one workflow that
requests the onboarding package.

39
00:02:29.485 --> 00:02:31.585
A mover on the organization code.

40
00:02:31.585 --> 00:02:34.537
And three leavers,
including a post-separation

41
00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:37.035
delete thirty days after the leave date.

42
00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:41.660
All of them scope on the employee type
and a worker type code, never on a name.

43
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.607
The day-one workflow
doesn't enable the account;

44
00:02:44.607 --> 00:02:48.310
the pipeline is that writer, as
Chapter 1's register records.

45
00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.940
And each file records its version,
owner and execution limit.

46
00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.527
The lint has fifteen rules, and
each one says where it comes from.

47
00:03:00.527 --> 00:03:04.370
Some are Learn's own limits:
the category, the offset range,

48
00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.293
more than twenty-five tasks,
more than a hundred workflows,

49
00:03:08.293 --> 00:03:12.053
an unknown task I D, or a scope
property Learn doesn't list.

50
00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:16.486
Some are the program's design: scope
by code rather than by a display name,

51
00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:20.017
record an execution limit
for every scheduled workflow,

52
00:03:20.017 --> 00:03:24.977
give access by package rather than group
add, and keep delete apart from disable.

53
00:03:24.977 --> 00:03:28.734
And the pass rule mixes both:
Learn's range is a finding,

54
00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:32.440
over twenty-four hours or
reusable is a warning.

55
00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.802
The plan is where the
file becomes a change.

56
00:03:38.802 --> 00:03:43.853
It lints the folder first, and refuses
to go on if any file has a finding.

57
00:03:43.853 --> 00:03:46.839
Then it matches each file
to the tenant's export by

58
00:03:46.839 --> 00:03:50.116
display name and compares
the parts a definition owns.

59
00:03:50.116 --> 00:03:52.878
A file with no match
becomes a create body.

60
00:03:52.878 --> 00:03:56.078
A changed one becomes a body
for create new version.

61
00:03:56.078 --> 00:03:58.428
An unchanged one is left alone.

62
00:03:58.428 --> 00:04:01.189
It writes the plan, one
body per change and a

63
00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:04.266
manifest with a hash per
file, and sends nothing.

64
00:04:04.266 --> 00:04:07.690
Its one read needs only
workflow read permission.

65
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.665
Readiness counts the hires
whose hire date is today,

66
00:04:14.665 --> 00:04:19.390
and calls a hire ready when the account
exists and is enabled, holds a license,

67
00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.740
has the onboarding package delivered,
and has a completed pre-hire run.

68
00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:29.390
It reads the hires from H R and three
exports, each with a read-only permission.

69
00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.357
Run it on an export taken
at the start of the day,

70
00:04:32.357 --> 00:04:35.090
because it measures state, not timestamps.

71
00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.929
The chapter adds nine tests,

72
00:04:36.929 --> 00:04:41.690
and all fifty-seven of the kit's
tests pass with no tenant or network.

73
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.140
The facts behind the code, read
on Learn on November first.

74
00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.684
Create and create new version share
one permission and the Lifecycle

75
00:04:53.684 --> 00:04:57.915
Workflows Administrator role, and
both are available at L4 and L5.

76
00:04:57.915 --> 00:05:00.723
Changes are versions,
not in-place updates,

77
00:05:00.723 --> 00:05:04.815
and the tenant runs one schedule,
every three hours by default.

78
00:05:04.815 --> 00:05:08.313
The pass task has a fixed
I D and a lifetime range,

79
00:05:08.313 --> 00:05:11.990
and its availability in
GCC High wasn't confirmed.

80
00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.690
Execution limits quarantine a
scheduled run but not an on-demand one.

81
00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:21.810
And a scheduled workflow catches up on a
user for three days after the trigger date.

82
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.326
Learn's own pages disagree in four places,

83
00:05:28.326 --> 00:05:31.498
recorded so a later edit
doesn't loosen the code.

84
00:05:31.498 --> 00:05:35.697
The pass's lifetime argument has two
names, and the lint reads either.

85
00:05:35.697 --> 00:05:38.685
The custom task extension has two I Ds.

86
00:05:38.685 --> 00:05:43.085
The mover category appears in an
example but not in the property table.

87
00:05:43.085 --> 00:05:45.761
And the workflow ceiling
is a hundred on two

88
00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:48.735
pages and fifty on another,
so the budget is fifty.

89
00:05:48.735 --> 00:05:52.668
Still open: the trigger name
for an extension attribute,

90
00:05:52.668 --> 00:05:58.670
the execution limit the API doesn't carry,
and the pass's availability in GCC High.

91
00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.270
What does this give the I S S O?

92
00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:08.206
The reviewed files and the plan
that changed each version,

93
00:06:08.206 --> 00:06:13.782
with its manifest hash: the definitions
and versions AC-2 enhancement 1 asks for.

94
00:06:13.782 --> 00:06:18.535
The lint's clean run, showing a one-time
pass of at most twenty-four hours

95
00:06:18.535 --> 00:06:23.370
and an execution limit on every
scheduled workflow, which supports IA-5.

96
00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.857
And the readiness summary
for each start date.

97
00:06:25.857 --> 00:06:30.413
The pass is a bridge to phishing-resistant
MFA, so an account still on a

98
00:06:30.413 --> 00:06:35.570
pass after the agreed window is a finding,
though this code doesn't read it.

99
00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:40.245
Who does what?

100
00:06:40.245 --> 00:06:45.282
Developers build the lifecycle automation,
and need to read Python and JSON.

101
00:06:45.282 --> 00:06:50.083
Identity engineers own the workflows:
they set each execution limit in the

102
00:06:50.083 --> 00:06:54.883
admin center, and send each change as
a Lifecycle Workflows Administrator.

103
00:06:54.883 --> 00:07:01.045
Reviewers approve each file and its plan,
and the I S S O receives the evidence.

104
00:07:01.045 --> 00:07:05.502
The code itself only reads: the planning
app holds a single workflow read

105
00:07:05.502 --> 00:07:10.460
permission, and the readiness reads
hold only their four read permissions.

106
00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.110
Lab 1 runs on a laptop.

107
00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.273
Step one lists the reads
and their permissions.

108
00:07:19.273 --> 00:07:24.047
The plan needs one read, of the
workflows, with workflow read permission.

109
00:07:24.047 --> 00:07:27.673
Readiness reads the users, the
pre-hire processing results

110
00:07:27.673 --> 00:07:31.522
and the package assignments, with
four read permissions in all.

111
00:07:31.522 --> 00:07:34.935
Step two lints the
program's six workflow files

112
00:07:34.935 --> 00:07:38.890
against a budget of fifty:
no findings, no warnings.

113
00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.387
Step three lints three broken copies,

114
00:07:45.387 --> 00:07:48.665
with a budget of two so
the ceiling rule fires.

115
00:07:48.665 --> 00:07:51.152
Seven findings and four warnings.

116
00:07:51.152 --> 00:07:53.974
The day-one copy has an
offset of two hundred

117
00:07:53.974 --> 00:07:56.639
days and adds the user to groups directly.

118
00:07:56.639 --> 00:08:01.125
The leaver triggers on the hire date,
deletes and disables in one workflow,

119
00:08:01.125 --> 00:08:04.140
and scopes on the manager,
which Learn doesn't list.

120
00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.819
The pre-hire copy has no execution
limit, scopes on a department name,

121
00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:12.078
and sends a three-day pass
that isn't one-time use.

122
00:08:12.078 --> 00:08:13.950
The exit code is two.

123
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.350
Step four plans against
the tenant's export.

124
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:23.985
The day-one workflow in the tenant
requests a different package,

125
00:08:23.985 --> 00:08:25.288
so it gets a new version.

126
00:08:25.288 --> 00:08:29.051
The employee leaver doesn't
exist yet, so it gets a create.

127
00:08:29.051 --> 00:08:33.800
The one warning is a legacy workflow
made in the portal that no file defines.

128
00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:34.975
Nothing is sent.

129
00:08:34.975 --> 00:08:37.600
Plan the broken folder and it refuses.

130
00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.200
Step five measures day one:
one of four hires ready.

131
00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.129
One lacks only the package,
one is disabled and unlicensed,

132
00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:47.012
and one has no account at all.

133
00:08:47.012 --> 00:08:49.410
The fifty-seven tests pass.

134
00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.947
Lab 2 uses a test tenant, never
production; the authors haven't run it.

135
00:08:57.947 --> 00:09:00.310
Export the workflows with a reader app.

136
00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.539
Make one example edit,
moving the pre-hire trigger

137
00:09:03.539 --> 00:09:06.847
from seven days to five,
and plan against the export.

138
00:09:06.847 --> 00:09:09.331
As a Lifecycle Workflows Administrator,

139
00:09:09.331 --> 00:09:12.910
send that one change with Graph
PowerShell, scheduling off.

140
00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.473
Set the execution limit
in the admin center,

141
00:09:15.473 --> 00:09:17.822
and read it back with the validation kit.

142
00:09:17.822 --> 00:09:20.660
Check the pass policy and
the manager's mailbox,

143
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.134
then measure readiness on the start date.

144
00:09:23.134 --> 00:09:25.932
Finally, back it out:
remove what you created,

145
00:09:25.932 --> 00:09:29.240
or send the previous body
as a further version.

146
00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:35.590
The dangers are mostly drift and scale.

147
00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:40.490
A workflow edited in the portal drifts
from its file; every plan warns on it.

148
00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.715
A scope on a department name silently
stops after a reorganization.

149
00:09:44.715 --> 00:09:48.490
A long or reusable pass
outlives the card's arrival.

150
00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.320
A rehire whose old account kept
its methods can't get a pass,

151
00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.315
so rehires need their own path.

152
00:09:54.315 --> 00:09:58.477
With no execution limit, a
bad H R load runs to the end.

153
00:09:58.477 --> 00:10:02.640
A delete in the leaver removes the
account before devices are retired.

154
00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.150
And a readiness figure from a midday
export counts late fixes as ready.

155
00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.250
Here's what done looks like.

156
00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.810
Every workflow in the tenant has a file,

157
00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.850
and the last plan showed
no unknown workflows.

158
00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.500
The lint is clean against
a budget of fifty.

159
00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:25.100
Every execution limit matches its
file, and the validation kit agrees.

160
00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:28.587
The pass is one-time and lasts
at most twenty-four hours.

161
00:10:28.587 --> 00:10:33.013
Each change reached the tenant as a
reviewed body with its hash recorded.

162
00:10:33.013 --> 00:10:35.463
The readers hold only read permissions.

163
00:10:35.463 --> 00:10:38.788
And the readiness rate is
reported for every start date.

164
00:10:38.788 --> 00:10:44.275
Next, Chapter 4: access from attributes,
and the mover's residual review.

165
00:10:44.275 --> 00:10:45.430
Thanks for watching.
